From: y52s recovery work
Subject: [PATCH] y52s: mint the profile auth token for the SID the key demands

Measured on PD2057 (Android 10, Trustonic keymaster 4.0):

  user 0 spblob key   USER_SECURE_ID = the .pwd-embedded handle   (works)
  profile key 999     USER_SECURE_ID = the plaintext .handle      (0x779b8423e32ee87)

so the tied-profile escrow key really is bound to the OTHER gatekeeper
enrolment of the parent.  The previous code tried to reach it by rewriting
userId inside a token that gatekeeper had minted for the first enrolment.
That cannot ever work: the hw_auth_token_t HMAC covers userId, so every one
of the 16 byte-order combinations was rejected with -26 by construction.

Do what AOSP does instead:

  * read the SID the key accepts from the key itself
    (getKeyCharacteristics -> KM_TAG_USER_SECURE_ID 502,
     plus USER_AUTH_TYPE 504 and AUTH_TIMEOUT 505), so no value is
    hardcoded and nothing depends on this device's numbers;
  * enumerate the gatekeeper handles the device stores
    (/data/system_de/<user>/spblob/<16 hex>.handle, one directory per user)
    and keep only the enrolment whose SID matches;
  * verify that enrolment with the owner credential and hand the token to
    begin() byte-for-byte, decoded by the same parser that already unlocks
    user 0;
  * fall back to the cached /auth_token when the key is not auth-bound or
    when no enrolled handle carries the SID (logged, never silent).

The tied-profile credential is now recovered for every profile that has a
lock file (the previous code assumed user 999), keyed by user id, while the
owner token is seconds old - the key only accepts a 30 s window.

Asserts that the strategy table is gone: rewriting a token's userId can only
produce -26, so keeping 17 attempts per boot was pure noise.

